<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rothenburg ob der Tauber</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rothenburg ob der <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauber">Tauber</a> is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, above the Tauber valley, which grew below a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> castle and was a free imperial city from 1274 until 1802. Taken by Tilly's imperial army in 1631, it then declined, which preserved its walled medieval townscape, now one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s best-known sights. The festival play of the Master Draught recalls the capture of 1631.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rothenburg-ob-der-tauber.jpg" alt="Rothenburg ob der Tauber" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berthold Werner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rothenburg_BW_4.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name means Red castle above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauber">Tauber</a> and describes the town's position on a plateau overlooking the Tauber river. Rothenburg Castle, also called the Alte Burg, gave the city its name. The counts of Comburg-Rothenburg built the castle in 1070, and the line died out in 1116 with Count Heinrich. Emperor Heinrich V then appointed his nephew Konrad von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> to succeed to their properties. Konrad, who became King Konrad III, built a Staufer castle on cheaper land nearby in 1142.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was founded in 1170, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Staufer</a> castle was built, around its marketplace and St. James' Church, and walls and towers followed in the 13th century. In 1274 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a> granted it privileges as a free imperial city, three fairs were established, and it grew.</p><p>The Heilig Blut pilgrimage attracted many pilgrims, and the city was one of the 20 largest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, with about 5,500 people within the walls and another 14,000 in the surrounding territory. Rabbi Meir ben Baruch of Rothenburg, who died in 1293, had a great reputation as a jurist in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. An earthquake destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Staufer</a> castle in 1356.</p><p>In October 1631, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, the Catholic Count of Tilly wanted to quarter his 40,000 troops in Protestant Rothenburg. The town resisted and was quickly defeated. According to the legend of the Meistertrunk, Tilly offered to spare the city if anyone could drink 3 1/4 litres of wine in one draught, and Mayor Georg Nusch did so; the story is almost certainly apocryphal, since it first appears in a chronicle written more than a century later. The troops left the town poor and nearly empty, and a bubonic plague in 1634 killed many more. Without money or power Rothenburg stopped growing and kept its 17th-century state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Rothenburg lies in Middle Franconia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and is on the Romantic Road. It is one of only four towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> with completely intact city walls. Painters of the Romantic period visited it from 1877, followed by the first tourists, and laws were passed to prevent major changes. On 31 March 1945 bombing destroyed around 275 houses and damaged the walls. S. Assistant Secretary of War John J.</p><p>McCloy ordered that artillery not be used, and the local commander Major Thömmes gave up the town, American troops occupied it on 17 April 1945. About 32 percent of the town was rebuilt, and rebuilt walls carry bricks naming donors. The Rathaus has a Gothic rear part of 1250 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> front begun in 1572, and St. James' Church holds a Holy Blood altarpiece by Tilman Riemenschneider.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rothenburg ob der Tauber” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Rothenburg</b> grew below a Hohenstaufen castle on a spur above the Tauber valley in the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-rothenburg">Free Imperial City of Rothenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>The Catholic Waldenburg branch included Schillingsfürst, on a hill near <b>Rothenburg</b>, and in 1744 its head was raised to the rank of prince of the Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hohenlohe-schillingsfurst">Principality of Hohenlohe-Schillingsfürst</a></li></ul></section>
